==Latin==
{{attention|la}}

===Etymology===
From  {{proto|Indo-European|dṓm|lang=la}}, from root {{proto|Indo-European|demh₂-|to build|lang=la|title=}}. Cognatels include Ancient Greek {{term|δόμος|sc=polytonic|tr=dómos|lang=grc}} and Sanskrit {{term|दम|tr=dáma|lang=sa|sc=Deva}}. The same [[Proto-Indo-European]] root also gave Old English {{term|timber||building, act of building|lang=ang}} (English {{term|timber|lang=en}}).

===Noun===
'''domus''' (''genitive'' '''domūs''' ''or'' '''[[domi|domī]]'''); {{f}}, ''[[Appendix:Latin fourth declension|fourth declension]]'' (''irregular'')

# a [[house]], a [[home]]{{jump|la|house|s}}
#* Motto of [[w:Methodist Ladies' College, Melbourne|Methodist Ladies' College, Melbourne]]
#*: ''Deo '''domui'''[[-que|que]].''
#*:: For God and for '''home'''.
#* First part of the motto of [[Harrow School]], [[England]]
#*: ''Stet fortuna '''domus'''.''
#*:: Let the fortune of the '''house''' stand.
# {{context|poetic|lang=la}} any [[building]] or [[abode]]
# one's [[native]] place, one's [[country]] or [[home]] (confer '''[[patria]]''')
# a [[household]], a [[family]], a [[race]]

